TenSaint73OS owner identity pin — template (English)

A public build ships without owner/OWNER.pin. While that file is absent or
contains no pin, `owner/import-owner-key` refuses every .tsowner import.
The pin is what tells the program which owner key file is genuine, so a
self-made key can never be imported into a build that carries your pin.

One-time registration (run it with the .tsowner you want to authorise):

    node owner/owner-vault.js fingerprint "/path/to/your.tsowner" --write

or, with the wrappers:

    sh owner/fingerprint-owner-key.sh "/path/to/your.tsowner"
    owner\Fingerprint-Owner-Key.bat "C:\path\to\your.tsowner"

The command reads the key, asks for its passphrase, and writes one line:

    OWNER_PIN=<64 lowercase hex characters>

Properties

1. The pin is derived from the key's username + password salt + password
   hash. It contains no password, no phone number, no plaintext and no
   part of the encrypted envelope.
2. The pin is portable. It belongs to owner/OWNER.pin inside the program
   directory, not to a computer. Copy the program folder to any device and
   the same .tsowner can be imported there. Commit the pin with your build
   if the build itself should accept that key on every device.
3. Import compares the pin with the fingerprint of the .tsowner being
   imported. The authorised file matches on every platform; any other key,
   including one generated by hand with valid JSON, does not match and is
   rejected.
4. The pin is an identifier, not a secret. Keeping it with the program is
   safe. Still, never publish the .tsowner itself, its passphrase, the
   data/ directory or an encrypted backup.
5. An existing, different pin is never overwritten. To rotate the owner
   identity, move owner/OWNER.pin aside on a machine you control, generate
   the new pin from the new key, and redeploy. Anyone able to edit the
   program folder is already trusted with the deployment.

Portable recovery on a new device

    copy the program folder (it contains owner/OWNER.pin)
    start it once so data/install.salt exists, then stop it
    run owner/import-owner-key and choose your .tsowner

If the same machine already holds the account data, copy data/ instead of
starting fresh; the imported identity keeps its own encrypted workspace and
existing members keep theirs.
